00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.090
Well, we'll be watching that.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:53.890
And then there are even discussions starting
about the possibility of states actually putting

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:56.010
in checkpoints at their own borders.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:02.630
Imagine, for example, that you have certain
states that are all in with we've got a distance,

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:05.290
whereas other states are all about let it
rip.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:09.750
You could conceivably have state governors
wanting to set up checkpoints at their borders,

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:14.670
which is a very complicated legal thing to
do in the United States.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:20.660
Freedom of movement between states is determined
by what's called the privileges and immunities

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:23.260
clause of the Constitution.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:28.150
And it is a pretty significant restriction
on states being able to erect barriers or

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:29.790
block entry to states.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.680
Free movement between states is in the Constitution.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.820
It's very difficult to do that.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:39.820
If it gets to that point, imagine that Louisiana
takes it seriously.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.520
But you know, Alabama doesn't or whatever.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:48.389
You could very quickly see that hypothetical
conflict become a very real legal conflict

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:49.840
in some of these states.

